==================================================================================
DOES THE PAIR-SUM RULER HAVE A MULTIPLICATIVE COUNTERPART?
==================================================================================
family                                          M      q  #pairs q|(vi+vj)   q | vi*vj too?
AP {1..12} u {13} (the tight 13-set)         1/14     14                 7              0/7
the wedge minimiser {1..11,13,84}            7/89     89                 1              0/1
covering-min {1..12} u {182}               14/183    183                 1              0/1
equioscillating {1..12} u {26}               2/27     27                 1              0/1
equioscillating {1..12} u {39}               3/40     40                 1              0/1
dilated 2*{1..12} u {13}                     1/13     13                 7              1/7

 READING: the ADDITIVE constraint q | v_i+v_j is by construction satisfied by every listed
 pair.  The MULTIPLICATIVE one q | v_i*v_j is satisfied only sporadically -- so there is NO
 automatic Vieta companion, and the pair is NOT pinned by the ruler alone.  That is a
 NEGATIVE and it is the useful content: THM-1002 gives one symmetric function, not two, so it
 buys a DISJUNCTION over candidate pairs rather than a determination -- structurally the same
 limitation as the e/pi argument.

==================================================================================
HOW MANY PAIRS SURVIVE?  (the size of the disjunction the ruler leaves open)
==================================================================================
  AP {1..12} u {13} (the tight 13-set)
     q = 14;  admissible pairs = [(1, 13), (2, 12), (3, 11), (4, 10), (5, 9), (6, 8)] ...
     their products = [13, 24, 33, 40, 45, 48, 49]   distinct products: 7
  the wedge minimiser {1..11,13,84}
     q = 89;  admissible pairs = [(5, 84)]
     their products = [420]   distinct products: 1
  covering-min {1..12} u {182}
     q = 183;  admissible pairs = [(1, 182)]
     their products = [182]   distinct products: 1
  equioscillating {1..12} u {26}
     q = 27;  admissible pairs = [(1, 26)]
     their products = [26]   distinct products: 1
  equioscillating {1..12} u {39}
     q = 40;  admissible pairs = [(1, 39)]
     their products = [39]   distinct products: 1
  dilated 2*{1..12} u {13}
     q = 13;  admissible pairs = [(2, 24), (4, 22), (6, 20), (8, 18), (10, 16), (12, 14)] ...
     their products = [48, 88, 120, 144, 160, 168, 169]   distinct products: 7

 So the ruler narrows the extremal pair to a SET, never to a point.  To pin the pair one
 needs a second, independent symmetric function -- exactly the ingredient the e/pi argument
 also lacks, and exactly the ingredient a one-parameter family supplies in THM-1550.


================================================================================
CORRECTION (klein-S353, on reading my own table)
================================================================================
 My summary line said the ruler "narrows the extremal pair to a SET, never to a point".
 THE TABLE SAYS OTHERWISE in 4 of 6 families.  The correct statement is a DICHOTOMY driven
 by the size of q relative to max(A):

   LARGE q -- {1..11,13,84} q=89, {1..12,182} q=183, {1..12,26} q=27, {1..12,39} q=40:
     exactly ONE admissible pair each.  The ruler PINS the extremal pair outright.
     Mechanism: v_i+v_j must be a multiple of q and at most 2 max(A), and when q is large
     only ONE multiple is reachable and only one pair attains it (e.g. 5+84 = 89).

   SMALL q -- tight AP {1..13} q=14, and its dilate 2*{1..12}u{13} q=13:
     SEVEN admissible pairs each (sum = q exactly: (1,13),(2,12),...,(7,7)).

 CONSEQUENCE, and it is the useful one: the pair-sum ruler is MOST informative exactly where
 the problem is HARDEST -- the wedge minimiser, the covering minimum, the equioscillating
 family are all pinned -- and LEAST informative on the tight AP, which is already settled by
 other means.  That inverts the usual pattern and is worth exploiting.

 The multiplicative finding stands: q | v_i*v_j holds in 0/7, 0/1, 0/1, 0/1, 0/1, 1/7 cases,
 so there is NO automatic Vieta companion to the additive ruler.
